h pleasure  anyway we don't really need to work any  harder making our lives more pleasant in  the sense of you know like for instance  I love to eat you know tasty food and I  know how to cook tasty food now I could  say I'm going to take some cooking  classes so I can learn how to make  sausages or learn how to really really  make good omelet or you know I love  pasta dishes maybe I take a cooking  class specifically on that do I have a  duty to improve my cooking abilities so  I can improve my my eating experiences  Ross would say come on now you know you  don't have a duty to improve your  enjoyment of cooking now you know what  about if I took a class where I actually  learn something about the properties of  the ingredients and how they fit in with  the tastes and it's a little bit more  you know science-based in terms of  what's going on with the food Ross would  say yeah okay but now you're talking  about intellectual improvement and not  just having a better time and say the  same thing about a wine tasting class  are you taking the wine tasting class  because you like drinking wine and  rolling it around your tongue and  hanging out with other people who like  to do that and you're like getting you  know getting sloshed because that's what  happens after a while if you swallow you  know even a little bit of it with a lot  of wine tasting what are you actually  learning something about the wine are  you improving your mind and even you're  improving your palate your your  sensitivity your ability to to  differentiate things  Ross would say those are very different  when it comes to improving our  situations with respect to pleasure  Ross would say really you don't have  much of a duty for that now you might  object and say wait a second what about  the kind of people who  characteristically deprive themselves of  pleasure who don't think enough about  that who never really explore anything  and whose lives kind of you know can  suck as a as a result they're they're  unpleasant lives they're lacking in  happiness that they could be enjoying  Ross would say that a person like that  would you know be able to see  self-improvement in terms of of that  that does make sense you know other  another thing that could come up can't  actually brings us up do you have a duty  to pursue your own happiness Khan says  not really except if being miserable  makes you less likely to actually do the  right thing  then yes you you do have a requirement  to to provide yourself with enough  happiness so that you're not going to be  a jerk to people Ansem actually says  something kind of interesting like that  about fasting you know in in the Middle  Ages fasting actually the ancient period  as well fasting was done by many people  who's seen as very important to the  monastic life because you need to get  control over your bodily appetites  before you can even start to work on  your own your screwed-up personality and  Ansem says some people really shouldn't  fast which it's kind of a strange thing  from a guy who did fast a lot and the  long student institution where fasting  is very important and he says here's why  you know some people are able to fast  and they do okay with it and and other  people it just makes them irritable and  if it's going to just make you irritable  better to like not be angry at people  because anger is worse than you know not  having control over your bodily  appetites he thinks better  to be irritable better not to be  irritable and break the fast even though  it be good for you to fast because  you're going to be irritable with  everybody else and that's not good for  you and it's not good for them so that  would be a case  sort of like what Cantus talking about  what Ross is talking about where yes  providing yourself with a certain  modicum of pleasure may allow you to  actually be a better person but there  we're talking about moral virtue in that  case the pleasure is a means not an  intrinsic good but i